A HashSet allows us to check for the existence of an element in **constant time $O(1)$** on average, which reduces the overall time complexity from $O(N \log N)$ (sorting) to **linear time $O(N + M)$**.

### Implementation

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CommonElementsOptimized {
    public static void findCommon(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        // Use a HashSet to store elements of the first array
        // HashSet provides O(1) average time complexity for additions and lookups
        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (int num : arr1) {
            set.add(num);
        }

        System.out.print("Common Elements: ");
        boolean found = false;

        // Iterate through the second array and check if the element exists in the set
        for (int num : arr2) {
            if (set.contains(num)) {
                System.out.print(num + " ");
                found = true;

                // Remove the element from the set to avoid printing 
                // duplicates if arr2 contains the same common element multiple times
                set.remove(num);
            }
        }

        if (!found) {
            System.out.print("None");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array1 = {1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 2, 1};
        int[] array2 = {2, 3, 5, 7, 2};

        System.out.println("Array 1: " + Arrays.toString(array1));
        System.out.println("Array 2: " + Arrays.toString(array2));

        findCommon(array1, array2);
    }
}
```

### Why this is the best approach

#### 1. Time Complexity: $O(N + M)$
*   **Building the Set:** We traverse the first array once to add all elements to the `HashSet`. This takes $O(N)$.
*   **Checking the Set:** We traverse the second array once. For each element, we perform a `.contains()` check and a `.remove()` operation. Both of these are $O(1)$ on average. This takes $O(M)$.
*   **Total:** $O(N + M)$. This is the theoretical lower bound because you must look at every element in both arrays at least once.

#### 2. Space Complexity: $O(N)$
*   We use a `HashSet` to store the elements of the first array. In the worst case (where all elements in `arr1` are unique), the space used is proportional to the size of the first array.
*   *Optimization Tip:* To further optimize space, you can check which array is smaller and put the **smaller array** into the `HashSet`. This ensures the space complexity is $O(\min(N, M))$.

### Comparison Table

| Approach | Time Complexity | Space Complexity | Note |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Brute Force** | $O(N \times M)$ | $O(1)$ | Extremely slow for large arrays. |
| **Sorting + 2 Pointers** | $O(N \log N + M \log M)$ | $O(1)$ | Best when memory is strictly limited. |
| **HashSet** | **$O(N + M)$** | $O(N)$ | **Fastest overall performance.** |

