const checkIfLastCharIsALetter = (txt) => {
  // Regular Expression explanation:
  // (^|\s)     : Matches either the start of the string (^) OR a whitespace character (\s)
  // [a-zA-Z]   : Matches any single alphabetical character
  // $          : Ensures this pattern occurs at the very end of the string
  const regex = /(^|\s)[a-zA-Z]$/;

  return regex.test(txt);
};

// Test cases
console.log(checkIfLastCharIsALetter("apple pie"));     // false
console.log(checkIfLastCharIsALetter("apple pi e"));    // true
console.log(checkIfLastCharIsALetter("apple pi e "));   // false
console.log(checkIfLastCharIsALetter(""));              // false
console.log(checkIfLastCharIsALetter("a"));             // true
```

### Explanation:
1.  **`(^|\s)`**: This is a grouping that looks for the "boundary" of a word. Since the prompt defines a word as characters separated by spaces, a letter is "not part of a word" if it is the only character in the string (start of string `^`) or if there is a space (`\s`) immediately before it.
2.  **`[a-zA-Z]`**: This ensures the character is an alphabetical letter.
3.  **`$`**: This anchors the search to the end of the string, ensuring we are only evaluating the last character.
4.  **`.test(txt)`**: This method returns `true` if the pattern is found and `false` otherwise.

